The proposed plan is good and follows the overall JWT migration strategy. However, before implementation I want to improve the architecture so that the User Service follows enterprise microservice best practices.

Please modify the implementation according to the following requirements before generating code.

===========================================================
GENERAL REQUIREMENTS
===========================================================

Analyze the complete user-service before making any changes.

Do NOT regenerate the project.

Do NOT rewrite unrelated code.

Modify ONLY user-service.

Do NOT modify auth-service or api-gateway.

===========================================================
IDENTITY
===========================================================

The frontend must NEVER send

authUserId

or any internal identifier.

The User Service must ONLY trust the following headers injected by the API Gateway.

X-User-Id

X-User-Email

X-User-Role

X-User-Authorities

Do not read any user identity from the request body.

===========================================================
CONTROLLER DESIGN
===========================================================

Keep business logic out of controllers.

Controllers should only

• Extract trusted headers

• Validate request

• Delegate to service layer

Authorization decisions should not be duplicated across multiple controllers.

Move reusable authorization logic into a dedicated authorization component or service where appropriate.

===========================================================
PROFILE APIS
===========================================================

Implement

POST /api/v1/users/profile

GET /api/v1/users/me

PUT /api/v1/users/me

DELETE /api/v1/users/me

These endpoints must always operate on the authenticated user obtained from X-User-Id.

The request body must never contain authUserId.

===========================================================
ADMIN APIS
===========================================================

Keep

GET /api/v1/users/{id}

PUT /api/v1/users/{id}

DELETE /api/v1/users/{id}

Restrict these endpoints to ROLE_ADMIN only.

Students and Instructors must never access another user's profile.

===========================================================
LISTING APIS
===========================================================

Review

GET /api/v1/users

GET /api/v1/users/search

These endpoints should not be public.

Restrict them to ROLE_ADMIN unless there is a specific business requirement.

===========================================================
SERVICE LAYER
===========================================================

The Service layer should remain responsible for business logic.

Controllers should never perform database-related authorization.

Pass the authenticated user information to the service layer.

===========================================================
AUTHORIZATION
===========================================================

Implement role-based authorization using Spring Security.

Do not rely only on manual role comparisons inside controllers.

Use proper security annotations where appropriate.

Ensure

ROLE_STUDENT

ROLE_INSTRUCTOR

ROLE_ADMIN

are consistently enforced.

===========================================================
VALIDATION
===========================================================

Ensure

A user cannot create more than one profile.

A user cannot update another user's profile.

A user cannot delete another user's profile.

Admin can manage every profile.

===========================================================
SWAGGER
===========================================================

Update Swagger documentation.

Document

/me

and

/profile

endpoints.

Document Bearer Authentication.

Clearly state that X-User-* headers are injected automatically by the API Gateway and must never be supplied manually by clients.

